===Personalized plates===
Alberta first issued personalized plates in 1985. As of 2018, over 80,000 such plates have been issued.<ref>{{cite web |title=Personalized Plates Program Information Sheet |url=https://www.servicealberta.ca/pdf/mv/Pers_Plate_Info_Sheet.pdf |website=Government of Alberta |publisher=Service Alberta |access-date=27 August 2018}}</ref>

Personalized plates for private passenger vehicles can feature between one and seven characters, including letters, numbers, and spaces, while plates for motorcycles and veterans' vehicles can feature between one and five characters. Special characters, such as hyphens and exclamation marks, are not permitted. As in other jurisdictions, combinations may be rejected or withdrawn by the Registrar of Motor Vehicle Services if they are deemed offensive or confusing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Snowdon |first1=Wallis |last2=Keeler |first2=Nola |date=27 August 2018 |title=Too rude for the road: Here are the licence plates Alberta won't allow |url=http://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/edmonton/edmonton-alberta-rejected-licence-plates-1.4797754 |publisher=[[CBC News]] |access-date=27 August 2018}}</ref>

Personalized plates are issued in pairs, except for motorcycles. Only the rear plate is required to be displayed.

===Specialty plates===
A specially designed Support Our Troops plate was introduced on July 22, 2014, to honor current and former members of the Canadian Armed Forces.<ref>{{cite web |title=Service Alberta: Support Our Troops Plate |url=http://www.servicealberta.com/2181.cfm |publisher=Government of Alberta |access-date=April 14, 2015}}</ref> The design features artwork in honour of the first four Alberta-based Canadian soldiers who died in Afghanistan in April 2002. The plate is available at registry agent locations in Alberta for a one-time $75 fee and a $9 registry agent service charge. For every plate purchased, $55 of the purchase price goes to the Support Our Troops Program managed by the Canadian Forces Morale and Welfare Services.<ref>{{cite web |title=Support Our Troops licence plate raised more than $220,000 |url=https://www.alberta.ca/release.cfm?xID=3731873E966F5-B6EB-BA5F-F362F26AD3A0CC08 |publisher=Government of Alberta |date=November 7, 2014 |access-date=April 14, 2015}}</ref>

On November 19, 2018, two specially designed [[Calgary Flames]] and [[Edmonton Oilers]] plates were introduced, with $55 of each $75 plate going to the Calgary Flames Foundation and the Edmonton Oilers Community Foundation.<ref>{{cite web |title=Licence plates have now joined the Battle of Alberta between Flames and Oilers |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/calgary/battle-of-alberta-licence-plates-1.4910441 |last=Dormer |first=Dave |publisher=Canadian Broadcasting Corporation |date=November 17, 2018 |access-date=November 18, 2018}}</ref><ref name=Plates2Months>{{cite web |title=When it comes to specialty licence plates, Edmonton Oilers winning Battle of Alberta |url=https://globalnews.ca/news/4890432/specialty-licence-plates-edmonton-oilers-calgary-flames-hockey/ |last=Mertz |first=Emily |publisher=Global News |date=25 January 2019 |access-date=27 January 2019}}</ref> In the first two months of sales 4507 plates were sold (2750 Oilers plates and 1757 Flames plates), raising over $247,000 for the foundations.<ref name=Plates2Months />

